# A prompt chunk's top-k pools a row (the indexer's top-512 of every visible pooled key) on a compacted
# radix select, with the same pools (TF_GLM_PROMPT_SELECT, default on; 0: top_pools as before):
#
# - _select_compact: the first two radix passes over every score; the columns at or above the 16-bit prefix
#   they find (the pools certainly chosen and the candidates, counted from the two histograms) are, when at
#   most 2,048, compacted in pool order by a third read, and the last two passes and the output read only
#   those. A row with more (many equal scores) reads every score as before. Three reads of a row's scores
#   instead of five; the same digits, pools above the threshold and lowest ties. It reads only the scores, so
#   it does not depend on the scoring kernel.
# - The scores stay 0086's _scores (one row a program, SCORE_LOOP pool blocks). The GLM-5.3 recipe's 0151 and
#   this port's first version also stacked two rows a tensor-core product (_scores_pair); that was written
#   against the scoring before 0086 and is left out until it can be measured against 0086.
#
# Not measured on this stack (the first version, with _scores_pair, took a 2,048-row chunk-layer at 8k / 32k /
# 128k tokens from 1.63 / 6.05 / 27.14 to 0.91 / 2.76 / 12.14 ms on one GB10). Flash has 11 such layers a
# chunk; with 0102 each rank selects its own rows only. Tests: tests/cpu/test_prompt_select.py (the same pools
# as top_pools with ties, -inf columns, rows of fewer visible pools than k, and the fallback over every score).
#
# Provenance and credit: the ideas of the GLM-5.3 recipe's patch 0151 (Mia's AI Lab), re-done for Flash's indexer
# (pooled keys, 4 rows a key tile already): its re-tiled scoring is after drowzeys' f0f382d
